WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
Could anime ever become mainstream?

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:09.700
back in 2013 I posed this question in a video Inspired by the overwhelming reach of shows like Attack on Titan

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.260
that had never quite been achieved before,

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.000
along with other key events like the unexpected revival of Toonami

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.000
and the success of The Little Witch Academia 2 Kickstarter campaign that signalled a possible change for the Industry

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.340
Within just these four years since then a lot has happened

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:28.080
Anime has seen unprecedented growth and recent events have come to light that I feel makes this question more relevant than ever

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:34.060
the medium has never been so close to mainstream as it is now so I thought it'd be the perfect time to revive this discussion,

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.940
and explore just exactly what has changed in just a few short years

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.700
Where the Industry is now and where it could possibly go in the future

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.200
Because, in my opinion that question has never been so exciting

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.940
Could anime actually be becoming mainstream?

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.320
Now, I know four Years Didn't Seem like too long time to re-evaluate this discussion, so to get an idea of just exactly how much has changed,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.020
for those of you that don't remember, let me paint the picture of what it was like back then

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.240
The Year is 2013

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:14.949
The eighth console generation had just started, Harlem Shake fever was taking over the internet and streaming services were just starting to pick up steam

00:01:15.369 --> 00:01:18.279
Crunchyroll were leading the pack in a big way but along with them we saw sites like

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:22.059
Daisuki, Animax, and Viewster trying to get their foot into the market to varying success

00:01:22.219 --> 00:01:24.879
See, before this the only real ways to consume media

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.259
Legally, came from waiting however many months or years it'd take for a show to get released on physical media

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:35.709
Which hardly anyone did. There was a gap, and the streaming industry was more than happy to fill this gap, being just on the cusp

00:01:35.709 --> 00:01:41.040
of booming as a whole. but we didn't know that at the time, it was a widely agreed sentiment that If anime were to ever

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:46.280
have the chance of bleeding into the mainstream, a television presence would have to play a big part in it

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:51.340
As stupid and outdated as that thought is now. But that was the climate's only four years ago

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.760
Mainstream hits were unheard of with a big three carrying the majority of that weight for years even with big hits within the community like

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:59.420
Madoka, Steins;Gate and Fate/Zero

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.989
Nothing looked even close to touching the popularity they had gained before, but with two of them coming to an end it was this period

00:02:04.989 --> 00:02:09.320
Where Sword Art Online and Attack on Titan made their presence known in a way nobody saw coming

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.940
Having shows get close to the level of popularity that Naruto and One Piece held was unheard of,

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.740
but managing to do that with a concise and easily digestible 25 episode package

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.360
just made them so much more accessible to larger audiences

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.980
Before this the whole seasonal anime culture was just starting to take shape

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.840
and i'd argue that it was these two shows, coupled with that ease of accessibility through streaming services

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.580
that had a few good years to put their foot on the ground, that got the ball rolling overall

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:36.310
Streaming, as it seems, was the way forward

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.680
There is no question how big a part streaming has played within these past few years for anime

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.640
if the boom in the early 2000s was due to the internet's giving increased accessibility

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:58.080
Leading to the rise of fansubs and torrents this era of streaming and simulcasting is where that accessibility reached critical mass

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.260
The one to most obviously benefit from this was of course, Crunchyroll

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.260
especially when you consider just how massive they've become at this point

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.300
and in turn how rapidly they have ascended to the forefront of online anime distribution

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.100
to think that back in mid 2012 when Sword Art Online started airing,

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.900
Crunchyroll had less than a hundred thousand paid subscribers

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.840
and since then they've experienced exponential growth every single year

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.340
Doubling to hit 200k by March 2013, Doubling Again to hit 400k by November 2014

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:31.480
Jumping to 700k by October 2015, and managing to hit a full million by February of this year

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:38.260
That's a tenfold increase in less than five years, which is an astounding progress as far as raw numbers are concerned

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.940
Every other competitor always seems to be just a step or two behind them and it's only

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:47.120
just recently with the intervention of multi billion mega corporations that we've actually seen some competiton

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.080
One of them of course being Netflix

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.860
Now, netflix saw Its own massive growth these past few years but instead of being in the Anime sphere,

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:59.660
it pioneered the way the populace consume media shifting us over from TV to online on-Demand streaming

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.600
Remember that outdated mentality that anime had no chance of being mainstream without television?

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:10.760
Well without Netflix leading that charge in streaming It still would be that way, which is why the company taking an active interest in

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.200
Anime may be one of the best things to happen to the industry

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.060
Maybe. We'll get back to that.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:21.080
Having mainstream megahits: Sao, Attack on Titan, One Punch Man, all available on netflix

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:27.060
only increased their spread, and it had people like Bill Burr tweeting about how he watched One Punch Man on that platform

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:33.060
But i'd be hard-pressed to assess the impact of streaming services for anime without acknowledging the other side of the spectrum:

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:37.080
Illegal sites. in the wake of the rise of internet streaming the once go-to Way

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:40.880
to pirate anime through fansubs and torrents has drastically reduced in popularity

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.120
in its place took the staggering growth in traffic for illegal streaming sites

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.700
Currently, the top illegal anime streaming site, which I respectfully won't name, but we all know what it is

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:56.860
has an Alexa global rank of 287 with around double the traffic that Crunchyroll gets

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:00.280
Granted Crunchyroll is more focused in the US but to put things into perspective,

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:04.120
It ranks higher than the likes of IGN, GameFAQs and Hulu

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:10.660
That's right, the biggest illegal streaming service for anime gets more traffic than two of the highest ranked of video game media outlets

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:15.220
and one of the biggest streaming services in america and that is just one site

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.740
whatever your opinion on illegal services is a topic for another video

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:22.920
but it's hard to deny the reach that streaming has given anime over these past few years

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:26.340
And that's not even all that's been happening. One thing that's really caught my attention

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:31.980
recently Is the opportunity to experience anime more commonly in a place that I never really could before

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.460
Aside from big franchise films it was rare for anime films to ever get screened anywhere

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:50.140
and while screenings were slowly popping up here and there the demand exploded last year with the release of Your Name

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:58.140
This film took the anime world by storm obliterating every kind of record in its path eventually overthrowing Spirited Away as the highest grossing anime film,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:02.380
And becoming the seventh highest grossing traditionally animated film of All Time

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.320
Beating out the likes of Disney classics like Pocahontas and Hunchback of Notre Dame

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.920
Within our community the buzz for it could not be higher, calling for one of the widest screenings i've seen here

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.540
in the west for a non franchise film, and in asia It was topping box offices left, right, and center

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:21.160
You couldn't jump in a London underground during that time without passing a good five Your Name posters before reaching your destination

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:25.660
And I hope what this really marks is the increased presence anime could have in theaters in the future

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:28.940
now there are more screenings than ever before If you live in populated areas

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:33.040
and even this is something I could never imagine just a few years ago and I hope

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:37.720
it's just a sign of things to come, but in the world of film, that's not all that's happening for anime

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.120
As it seems like Hollywood could also be taking interest. If you look at the comic book industry,

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.160
one thing that's catapulted comics into mainstream success is the current obsession Hollywood has with comic book films

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:51.120
and recently it seems like some of that attention has turned to anime with the continual announcements of,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.380
love them or hate them

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.360
Live-Action Adaptations. Naruto, Cowboy Bebop,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:01.780
Sword Art Online, even fucking One Piece, all these shows have western adaptations in the work whether you think it's a good idea or not

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:05.380
and this is ignoring Ghost In The Shell which came out earlier this year

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:10.560
It's a far cry from getting an Mcu mega hits just yet or even getting a single decent adaptation,

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.580
But surely one of these days Hollywood will learn how to do an anime adaptation done right

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:17.280
Don't quote me on that

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.240
Looking at things like Netflix's upcoming live-action Death Note

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:26.860
It may seem weird to have a foreign take on a Japanese IP, but this mix of culture could be something we'll be seeing more of in anime itself

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:31.200
Because more and more anime is becoming a Global Industry

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:46.420
Back in the day we could only speculate how much of an impact we had overseas

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:50.600
or how much shows like Cowboy Bebop were really made with an international market in mind

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.900
with It being a seemingly impossible idea that the west could ever be able to collaborate with japanese companies

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:59.400
Beyond big-budget high-profile projects such as The Animatrix

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:06.360
then on January 4th 2014 a certain show premiered in a fashion that nobody could have expected

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:19.260
The successful revival of Toonami was not only unexpected for the reignited interest of a dead timeslot,

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:25.820
But the premiere of Space Dandy on it set a landmark that had never been achieved before not only was it being broadcasted with a dub

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:32.620
Simultaneously to it's japanese release but the official premiere of the show was broadcasted in America before It was in Japan

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:38.320
This was unheard of, in a time when you'd usually have to wait almost a year to hear the dub release,

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.040
here was a show that premiered in the west before it did in Japan in a Foreign language no less

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:46.600
And while the show itself didn't leave a massive mark in the community that year

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:51.900
it was a small sign that Japanese companies were willing and interested to collaborate with outside markets

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:56.920
Fast-Forward to today and we now not only have concrete data of this but we're at a point where we can say that foreign companies

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:01.300
have an active involvement in the anime making process. The Canipa Effect has done a great

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:05.820
in-depth breakdown of just how global the medium of anime has become not only for companies involved

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.980
but also for individual staff members who work on anime

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:11.860
the days of anime being exclusively produced domestically in Japan,

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:16.240
only to be licensed and distributed down the line to other countries are slowly coming to a close

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.040
It's no secret that a lot of animation is outsourced to other asian countries like korea and China,

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:25.000
and with heavy international involvement of recent productions coming to fruition on top of that,

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.700
it's clear that the global market for our beloved medium is booming

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:31.920
More and more we are seeing the gap closing between the Japanese and International industry

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.200
You have foreign animators given key positions in anime productions

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:40.280
Crunchyroll went from simply licensing anime to now becoming a part of the production committee to fund anime

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:44.960
Finally bearing fruit with the release of Masamune-kun's Revenge and Kemono Friends this year

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:50.020
Creator of The Boondocks LeSean Thomas has also led the charge with shows of truly international collaborations

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:54.940
Firstly would be Canon Busters which was animated by studio Satellite and more recently with Children of Ether,

00:09:55.100 --> 00:10:00.860
The world's first Crunchyroll original anime, That Includes key star from big names who have worked in some massive titles

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.400
And animated by a studio with ties in both Japan and France

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:08.980
On top of this let's not forget that one sensation that blew through the internet's last year

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.240
Yes i'm sure Shelter is still very fresh in our memories

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:22.400
With it just coming out under a year ago, while also being the sole cause of death to an r/anime mod every time it's mentioned

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:26.460
Though Shelter was just a six minute animated Music video, it represented a lot more

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:32.700
Being the passion project of Porter Robinson, who, with help of Crunchyroll and A-1 pictures turned his vision into a reality

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.540
He showed us with the right amount of passion, funding, determination and connections,

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.260
A simple fan of anime could make their own anime

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:43.160
the unreachable dream many of us thought would be impossible had happened

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.740
Which leads us to the industry today

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:59.020
2017 seems to be the time when all these events have come together into overdrive,

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:01.940
and I get the feeling we could be on the cusp of something big

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:07.900
We've gotten to the point where the industry has garnered the attention of multinational media titans who are investing heavily in it

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:12.220
Amazon launched Anime Strike which while just a joke for a lot of the community when it first launched,

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:17.000
has continually acquired many of the best shows every season, to the dismay of many fans

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:22.860
Sony has acquired majority stakes in Funimation in a move that has left fans excited but cautious at the same time

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:26.900
And netflix most of all, seems to be interested in heavily investing in anime

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:31.700
after announcing that surprisingly 90% of anime viewed on that platform comes from outside Japan,

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:34.020
They announced ten new original anime series

